    title: "Farming Above the City",
    sourceMeta: "Student draft essay (original) · Original passage written for DrillMaster",
    body: "Read this passage and answer the questions that follow.\n\nFarming Above the City\n\n(1) When most people picture a farm, they imagine open fields, red barns, and long rows of crops stretching toward the horizon. (2) In New York City, however, some of the freshest vegetables grow several stories above the street. (3) On the flat roofs of warehouses, schools, and apartment buildings, city farmers are planting tomatoes, lettuce, peppers, and herbs. (4) Rooftop farms are spreading in cities around the world because they help solve several problems at once.\n\n(5) One reason for their popularity is that they bring fresh food closer to the people who eat it. (6) Much of the produce sold in city supermarkets travels hundreds of miles from the farms where it was grown. (7) By the time it reaches the shelf, it may have lost some of its flavor and nutrients. (8) Vegetables from a rooftop farm can be picked in the morning and sold at a neighborhood market that same afternoon. (9) In some neighborhoods, fresh fruits and vegetables is hard to find, so a nearby rooftop farm can make a real difference.\n\n(10) Rooftop farms also help the environment. (11) Plants and soil soak up rainwater, which means less water rushes into city sewers during heavy storms. (12) A layer of plants also shades the roof, the building below stays cooler during hot summer months. (13) In addition, rooftop gardens give bees, birds, and butterflies a nice spot in the middle of all that stuff.\n\n(14) Building a rooftop farm is not simple. (15) Engineers must first make sure a roof is strong enough to hold the heavy weight of soil, water, and equipment. (16) Farmers also need a reliable way to pump water up to the roof so that the plants do not dry out. (17) Many rooftop farmers say the effort is worth it. (18) For this reason, some farms use lightweight growing materials instead of ordinary soil.\n\n(19) Rooftop farms can also become outdoor classrooms where students see how a seed turns into food. (20) As cities search for ways to become healthier and greener, more people are likely to start looking up.",
